WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
The great resignation
is continuing to loom.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.110
There are more than 11 million
unfilled jobs right now.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.650 align:start position:0% line:0%
We hear from businesses
all over the country,

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.810
that it's very hard to hire people.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.620
Almost two years into the pandemic,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.030 position:63%
workforce participation remains slumped,

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.700 position:64%
while job openings are near record highs.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.320
And, this is coming at a time
when the recruiting process

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.940 position:66%
is going through a radical transformation.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:24.320
Companies in the past

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.560
who were maybe getting
dozens of applicants

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.190
for a single vacancy.

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:30.110
Now, they're getting hundreds,

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.620 position:58%
maybe even thousands of applications.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.020
And, so, software has come
in to automate the process

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.490
of looking through all of
these different candidates.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.530
If you've applied for a
job in the last five years,

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:44.110
you've probably been
impacted by this technology,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.230 position:59%
whether it was screening your resumes,

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.000
making you play games to
analyze your personality,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.660
making you go through some
other automated system.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.840
And, some people are fighting back.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.940 size:88% position:44% line:0%
Who's accountable when those
AI machines break the law?

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.160 align:start position:0% line:0%
Why it's important that
Congress take action

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.620 align:start position:0% line:0%
to ensure that big tech
is held accountable.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.035 size:78% position:39% line:0%
They claim there is labor
shortages, but there is

00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:10.837 align:start position:0% line:0%
a plethora of people out there
who are looking for jobs .

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:15.265
We're at a moment of growing
crisis with AI hiring.

00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:17.290 position:63%
And, that's why people are eager to act.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.940 position:56%
Amidst this whirlwind of disruption,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.590
one has to ask the question,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.820 position:64%
how did we go from this to this, to this?

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.230
More than a decade ago,

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:38.680
instead of just replying
to a job in a newspaper,

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:40.840
printing out hundreds of resumes,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.370
and sending them out
physically to companies,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.110 size:81% position:41% line:0%
now you can apply to even
more companies all at once

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.082 line:0%
by doing it over the internet.

00:01:49.082 --> 00:01:50.142
If you hate going to work

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:51.640 position:58%
and your coworkers don't respect you.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.473
Hey, dummy.

00:01:52.473 --> 00:01:54.740 position:63%
It's probably time - you need a new job.

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:55.730
Companies now,

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.160
they're getting hundreds,

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.650 position:58%
maybe even thousands of applications.

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.160
And, so, software has come
in to automate the process

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.560
of looking through all of
these different candidates,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.110 line:0%
effectively using something called ATS

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.830 line:0%
or applicant tracking systems.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:12.430 size:84% position:42% line:0%
These systems effectively
track different applicants,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.620
look through their resumes,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.470
and take them even potentially
through the whole process.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.200
They will take candidates
who are at the top,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.260 position:63%
the middle, and the bottom of performers

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:26.550
as deemed by a particular
company or hiring company,

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:29.950
and then train their systems
based on those judgments.

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.100
And, then, use that as a baseline

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.180
on which to make it's
own automated judgements

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.300 position:58%
about whether someone is good or bad.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.210
Not just students,
experienced professionals.

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.010 line:0%
They just don't know that these tools

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.540 align:start position:0% line:0%
are used so frequently.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.600
Gracy Sarkissian

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.860 position:64%
runs New York University's career center,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.410
where she gives tips to
optimize applications

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:53.930
for AI systems.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.970
Students will encounter
AI tools in an interview,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:58.440 position:58%
if they're doing a virtual interview.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.220
Some folks will also counter
AI powered assessments

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.370
as part of their interview process,

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.330
depending on the industries
in which they're entering.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.010
Well, the use of the
software is very prevalent.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:10.130
There was a recent Harvard
business study that came out

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.340
showing nearly every single one
of the Fortune 500 companies

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.110
use this kind of software.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:18.280
Large companies are never gonna
be able to stop using this.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:19.790
They just have no choice,

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:23.590
because of the inbound
flood of applicants they get

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:24.543
every single day.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.980 line:0%
Most vendors position their software

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.847 align:start position:0% line:0%
as something that can
alleviate the human bias,

00:03:29.847 --> 00:03:32.930 align:start position:0% line:0%
which has shown to be endemic
to the hiring process.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.070 line:0%
But, critics question whether AI

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.790 line:0%
is actually injecting fairness

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.250 size:78% position:39% line:0%
or just making those same
biases more efficient.

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.730
I definitely think there is
a segment of the population

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:47.560 align:start position:0% line:0%
that is being excluded
or not fully represented.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.460 line:0%
And, they are being left to the wayside.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:50.710
This is Jamaal Eggleston.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.690
He's a work readiness
instructor at the Hope Program,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.050
a nonprofit that helps
New Yorkers find jobs.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:58.930
My students aren't prepared for it.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.040
And, coming from a population
that might not be, again,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.900
as familiar with the technology,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.160
they run into pitfalls,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.480
and they don't do as
well as other communities

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.623
when encountering these AI.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.790 position:64%
Since US companies don't have to disclose

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.160
when they're using AI,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.110 position:64%
many applicants are still writing resumes

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:18.630
catered to the human eye,

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.963
rather than to AI.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:23.150
When people tell you

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:25.320
that you should dress
up your accomplishments

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:27.500 align:start position:0% line:0%
or should use non-standard
resume templates

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.010 line:0%
to make your resume stand out,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.760 line:0%
when it's in a pile of resumes,

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.280 align:start position:0% line:0%
that's awful advice.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.280
The only job your resume has

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.330
is to be comprehensible
to the software or robot

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:40.520
that is reading it,

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.810
because that software
or robot is gonna decide

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:46.040
whether or not a human
ever gets their eyes on it.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.130
Ian Siegel runs ZipRecruiter,

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:49.860
one of the dozens of AI companies

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.840
which transformed the
job hunt in recent years.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.550
You want to write your resume
for robots, not for humans.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.550 position:56%
These softwares not only review them

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.200 position:58%
to decide who gets passed to a human,

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.350
but another thing they do is
they write simple summaries

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:02.760
of who you are for those humans,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.080
so that those humans may, in fact,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.840
never see your actual resume.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.310
And, it's not just resume screening

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:09.150
that has changed.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.010 position:59%
Initial job interviews and assessments

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.600
are being automated at
companies like Goldman Sachs,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.313
Delta Airlines, and Walmart.

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:20.770
This screen may look familiar.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.770
You have a minute or
two to answer questions

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.090
or play an assessment game,

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.230
which will determine whether or not

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.100
you move on to the next round.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.290
So, we decided to try
out one of these systems

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:30.260
for ourselves.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.936
Talk about a time when I
was overwhelmed or stressed.

00:05:33.936 --> 00:05:36.604 position:58%
I had an employee who's working style

00:05:36.604 --> 00:05:39.790 position:64%
and my management style were not in sync.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.250
Alright, playing the first game,

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.970
it's kind of like a
remembering, memorizing.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.690
Four, six, seven.

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:47.523
And, you have to, okay,

00:05:47.523 --> 00:05:49.250
so it's like pattern recognition.

00:05:50.448 --> 00:05:52.790
Oh.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:55.300 position:56%
Okay, maybe I'm not getting the job.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:56.250
I don't know how much this

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.267
is going to tell about a person.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:02.303
What?

00:06:02.303 --> 00:06:03.710 position:64%
Okay, your assessment has been submitted.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.123
Please, click the button
above to continue.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.770
While our tests were unscored,

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.950
many companies algorithmically rank

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:15.550
interviews based on
word choice, vocal tone,

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:16.933
and facial expression.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.460
A lot of people are missing out.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.410
A lot of people are not giving back

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:22.840 position:56%
what the interviewer is looking for,

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.590 position:63%
because it was programmed a certain way.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:26.810
These things are programmed
by a certain segment

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:28.010
of the population

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.900
that might not be totally inclusive

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.820
or not be fully aware of
how other cultures emote.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.690
I believe race is a part of it.

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.170
I know it's trying to be...

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.900
Trying to leave race out of it.

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.010 position:56%
I know it's trying to be not biased,

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:43.110
but some things are inherent,

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:44.830
even if it's unconscious.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.820
I like to call it the
ghost in the machine.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:48.653
In a statement,

00:06:48.653 --> 00:06:51.210
HireVue said its systems
are rigorously audited

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.550
for adverse impact,

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:53.900
meet all regulations,

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.260
and only analyze word choice.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:57.093
Last year,

00:06:57.093 --> 00:06:59.600
the company announced it
discontinued facial analysis,

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.736
in part over bias concerns.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:05.970
Applicants trying to navigate
this new world of hiring

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.720
find themselves looking
for help on social media.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.360
This is how to beat automated
resume screening software.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.640
Here's how to trick the
online job application.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.350
If you know how to play the game,

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.630 position:63%
you're actually able to stand out on top

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:19.110
as the best candidate.

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:20.660
One controversial strategy

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.660
is copying an entire job
description into your resume,

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:24.890
hidden with white font,

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:28.120
in order to trick the AI
into picking up keywords.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.170 position:61%
They won't be able to see that you have

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.640
all of those qualifications

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.840
and its exactly what they
have on their website,

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.003
because it's all white.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.250
It's eventually going
to go to a human being,

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.350 position:56%
that's going to look at that resume,

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:40.670
and say, this is ridiculous.

00:07:40.670 --> 00:07:42.250
It's not about beating the system.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:46.190
I think that that thinking
is not gonna help candidates

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:47.400
to be most successful.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.790
What helps you be most
successful in the job search

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:53.130
is to know that the world
of work is always changing.

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:54.330
There are tech vendors

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:57.000
that help candidates optimize
their resume for systems.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.100
We have a tool that we use at NYU,

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.180
other universities use it,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.200
it's called VMock.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:03.460
Students can put their
resume into the system

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.880
and it will give them a score.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.140
And, one of the things
that it's scoring for

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.280
is how likely it will be
to be picked up by an ATS

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.853
for a particular industry.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:15.040 position:56%
While elite universities have access

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.100
to resume optimizing tech,

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:19.930
others may be left behind
in this new world of work.

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.810
There are millions of people

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.550 position:64%
who are being left out of the job market,

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.260
because hiring software is
discounting them very early on

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.093
on the process,

00:08:29.093 --> 00:08:30.920
when it scans their resumes.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.200
We have students who...

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.590
Some had bouts with incarceration,

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.170 position:66%
some of our students are college students,

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:38.250 position:66%
some of our students are long-term workers

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:40.570
who have been laid off
because of the pandemic.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.590
And, these are good people
who have great experiences,

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.040
who can add and become assets
to a lot of these companies.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:47.920
For example,

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.350
a person who, perhaps,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.440
spent some time in the military

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.490 position:58%
or someone who went on parental leave

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:56.870
might have a year or two
long gap in their resume.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:59.410
Now, if you were going to
interview with a human being,

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.120
you might be able to explain
why that gap is there,

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.390
but an algorithm doesn't
necessarily see that.

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.810
It just sees that,
between a couple of years,

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:08.090
you weren't working.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.000 position:58%
A lot of times, they get no response.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.925
And, it's really hard,
because there are...

00:09:11.925 --> 00:09:13.970 position:56%
They don't know what they did wrong.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:15.520
They don't know if they
did anything wrong.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:16.353
There's this term,

00:09:16.353 --> 00:09:18.580 position:64%
the black box of artificial intelligence.

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.440
What's happening with
some of these algorithms

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.920
is they might be discounting

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.820 position:59%
hundreds of different types of people,

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:26.883
but even the people who create
the software don't know why.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:29.208
In a statement,

00:09:29.208 --> 00:09:31.640 position:59%
icims, one of the leading ATS vendors,

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.040 position:61%
said it takes these concerns seriously,

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:36.430 position:64%
and has a team focused on responsible AI,

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:39.083
and follows industry
initiatives on fairness.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.080
Algorithmic discrimination
is difficult to prove,

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.320
but it's been revealed by audits.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.490
Amazon scrapped its AI
recruitment tool in 2018,

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.960
after it discriminated
against applications

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.350 position:63%
which listed women's colleges or sports.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:57.660 align:start position:0% line:0%
In another system,

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.290 line:0%
the best predictors of job performance

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.090 size:84% position:42% line:0%
included being named Jared,

00:10:01.090 --> 00:10:02.810 line:0%
or having played high school lacrosse,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.690 line:0%
according to an auditing law firm.

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.580
The algorithms that are being used

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.040
to look at people's resumes,

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:11.480
to conduct interviews,

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:15.430
they've been trained on data
about previous applicants,

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.030
is over perhaps the past decade.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:20.030
That's how machine learning works.

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.310 position:56%
No one explicitly told these systems

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.143
to discriminate,

00:10:23.143 --> 00:10:24.520
but they were trained on millions

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.470
of biased hiring decisions.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:28.680
The fear with these algorithms

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.170
is that you take bias data in,

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.040
because they're using
the wisdom of the crowd

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.310
and the crowd is clearly biased,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:38.010
and what you're really
doing is perfecting bias.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:40.400 position:61%
That's the accusation that's been made.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.270
The reality is that when
we deploy these algorithms,

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:46.140
we, as the software
engineers and programmers,

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.480
get to make choices about
what data the algorithm

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.010
can and cannot use.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.160
We can say to the algorithm,

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.050 position:56%
you never get to see someone's name.

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.280
If I tell you someone's
name is Jeremy Levine.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:56.760
You know something about them.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.080 position:64%
If I tell you somebody's name is Natasha,

00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:00.660
you know something about them.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:01.890
And, by removing them,

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.960
we create the most level
playing field we possibly can,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.660
so as much as possible,

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.273
it's a merit-based system.

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.270
While automated hiring tools

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:14.500
have become ubiquitous,

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.720
public perception hasn't caught on.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.180
Recent Pew surveys found that

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.020
most Americans didn't know resumes

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.740
are being screened by computers

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.280
and most considered the
practice unacceptable.

00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:27.280
But, one crucial group is
paying attention to AI,

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.170
lawmakers.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:30.760
The European union has
unveiled its proposed strategy

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.470
for artificial intelligence,

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.760
including regulations on
transparency and data protection.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.500 line:0%
Concerns bout how this technology

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.710 line:0%
could exacerbate discrimination,

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:41.470 line:0%
for the FTC, I think, foremost,

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:42.910 line:0%
the FTC needs to be making sure

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.810 align:start position:0% line:0%
that we're fully
understanding this technology.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:47.500
A federal civil rights agency

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.170 position:61%
is scrutinizing automated hiring tools,

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:52.350
while Illinois and DC have explored

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.440
some of the nation's first
AI hiring regulations.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.880
The Federal Trade Commission
recently gave a message

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.540
to companies using AI,

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:00.880
hold yourself accountable,

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.120 position:64%
or be ready for the FTC to do it for you.

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:03.953 align:start position:0% line:0%
In New York,

00:12:03.953 --> 00:12:08.810 size:84% position:42% line:0%
we just enacted one of the
first AI hiring regulations

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:09.880 align:start position:0% line:0%
in the country.

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:11.200
Albert Fox Cahn

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.150
runs a technology advocacy group

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:15.530 line:0%
and participated in New York's task force

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:18.480 line:0%
to understand the impact of AI systems.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.840 position:64%
The bill basically started off by saying,

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:22.960
before you sell this technology,

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.280
you'll have to do an audit,

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.990
and you'll have to tell people

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.010
when this technology has been used.

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:30.810 position:64%
And, when you look at what this bill did,

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.380
it was regulation in name only.

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.780 line:0%
Having an audit can be useful,

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:36.270 size:84% position:42% line:0%
but when that audit is done

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:38.840
by the software vendor themselves,

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:41.180
well, that's not worth
the paper it's written on,

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.200
and it's absurd.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.730 position:63%
But, in the New York law, you can't sue.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:49.610
It doesn't matter how
egregious the software is.

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:51.800
It doesn't matter how biased.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.160
You don't get your day in court.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.640
And, without that, none
of these bills can work.

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:58.580 align:start position:0% line:0%
In response,

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:00.250 line:0%
some of the world's largest companies

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.620 line:0%
like IBM and Facebook parent Meta,

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:05.770
have devised an auditing
standard for AI and hiring.

00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:08.190
However, critics say
that industry standards

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.463 position:64%
can't replace true government regulation.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.920 position:64%
We don't trust companies to self-regulate

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:16.160
when it comes to pollution,

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:17.750 position:56%
we don't trust them to self-regulate

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.120
when it comes to workplace comp,

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:23.980
why on earth would we trust
them to self-regulate AI?

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:26.820
Look, I think a lot of the
AI hiring tech on the market

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:28.100
is illegal.

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:29.410
I think a lot of it is biased.

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.890
I think a lot of it
violates existing laws.

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.510 position:61%
The problem is you just can't prove it,

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:38.150
not with the existing laws
we have in the United States.

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:41.300 position:66%
So, a big part of this is playing catch up

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.130
to open up discovery,

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:44.490
to open up the court process,

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:47.823
to say you can look at these
algorithms more easily.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.510
With widespread adoption,

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:54.080
the use of AI hiring tools
is less the future of work

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:55.560
and more like the present.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.350
Virtual assessments, interviews,

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.350
and job applications likely
aren't going anywhere,

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:01.410
but it's up to the employers

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:02.940
to decide how much of their process

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.090
they really want to automate.

00:14:05.090 --> 00:14:07.840
Smaller companies have a
little bit more choice here,

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.850 position:64%
because they don't get as many applicants

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:11.290
as the big companies do.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.010 position:64%
So, one thing that's been suggested to me

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:14.630 position:58%
by people in the recruitment industry

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:18.280
is actually maybe the small
to medium-sized businesses

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:21.050 position:64%
should just be a little bit more critical

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:21.883
in their thinking

00:14:21.883 --> 00:14:24.190
of whether they really
want to use this software.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:25.340
Because, in some cases,

00:14:25.340 --> 00:14:28.480
just using a human being
to interview someone,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.760 position:56%
even to look through all the resumes

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.280
might stand a better chance
of finding that star,

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:37.010
that star employee that
they're looking for.

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:41.410
Keep a segment or a avenue
open for an older person,

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:43.190
for a person who was incarcerated,

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:44.660
for a person who's young,

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:47.010
for a person who's just not
aware of these technologies,

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:50.280
so they can also have an
opportunity to get hired.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:53.950
We can't solely base
something as important

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.500
as finding a job,

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.290
as finding a career,

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:59.320
to only to AI.

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:01.660 position:56%
It's hard to fight against something

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:04.030
that has been programmed
one way or the other.

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:07.463
We can only teach our students
how to navigate these waters.

